    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d86">

                <pb facs="0136" n="125" sameAs="frus:frus1937v02/pg125"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.0141 Phoenix Group/1</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Secretary of the
                        Navy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Swanson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-03-09">March 9, 1937</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The receipt is acknowledged of a letter
                    dated February 20, 1937, from the Acting Secretary of the Navy, in which the
                    Department is requested that diplomatic notification be made regarding a visit
                    of the U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Avocet</hi> to Enderbury or Canton
                    Islands in the Phoenix Group for the purpose of transporting an observation
                    party of scientists.</p>
                <p>Inasmuch as this Government has not recognized the sovereignty of any other
                    country over the islands in the group in question the Department is not in a
                    position to give notification to any foreign power of a contemplated visit
                    thereto by a United States naval vessel for any purpose.</p>
                <p>Moore’s International Law Digest, <ref target="frus:frus1937v01/pg_567">Volume I,
                        pages 567</ref> and <ref target="frus:frus1937v01/pg_568">568</ref>, gives a
                    list of Guano Islands appertaining to the United States issued by the Treasury
                    Department in the middle of the nineteenth century. <pb facs="0137" n="126"
                        xml:id="pg126"/> It will be noted that most of the islands in the Phoenix
                    Group including Enderbury and Mary’s (Canton), appear in the list, and since
                    official records disclose that a certificate was issued for Enderbury Island by
                    the Department on December 31, 1859, and that the island was occupied and
                    exploited under that authority, this Department perceives no reason why the U.
                    S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Avocet</hi> should not visit Enderbury Island.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed>R. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Walton Moore</hi></signed><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Assistant
                        Secretary</hi></closer>
            </div>
